Increase the difficulty of the given programming test by requiring the use of multiple semaphores to synchronize access to multiple shared variables.

Rewrite the code such that it uses two semaphores, `semaphoreX` and `semaphoreY`, to synchronize access to the shared variables `x` and `y`, respectively. The program should ensure that only one thread can increment `x` at a time, and only one thread can increment `y` at a time, preventing race conditions.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;

public class ThreadSafeIncrement {
    private static int x = 0;
    private static int y = 0;
    private static Semaphore semaphoreX = new Semaphore(1);
    private static Semaphore semaphoreY = new Semaphore(1);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
                incrementX();
                incrementY();
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
                incrementX();
                incrementY();
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        try {
            t1.join();
            t2.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        System.out.println("Final value of x: " + x);
        System.out.println("Final value of y: " + y);
    }

    private static void incrementX() {
        try {
            semaphoreX.acquire();
            x++;
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            semaphoreX.release();
        }
    }

    private static void incrementY() {
        try {
            semaphoreY.acquire();
            y++;
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            semaphoreY.release();
        }
    }
}
```

In this rewritten test, two semaphores, `semaphoreX` and `semaphoreY`, are used to synchronize access to the shared variables `x` and `y`, respectively. Each semaphore is initialized with a single permit, ensuring that only one thread can acquire the semaphore at a time. The `acquire()` method is called before incrementing the respective variable, and the `release()` method is called in a `finally` block to ensure the semaphore is always released, even if an exception occurs. This ensures that only one thread can increment `x` at a time, and only one thread can increment `y` at a time, preventing race conditions.

