Increase the difficulty of the given programming test by requiring the use of a specific synchronization mechanism, such as locks or semaphores.

Rewrite the code such that it uses a lock to synchronize access to the shared variable `x` and prevents race conditions.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class ThreadSafeIncrement {
    private static int x = 0;
    private static Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
                increment();
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
                increment();
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        try {
            t1.join();
            t2.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        System.out.println("Final value of x: " + x);
    }

    private static void increment() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            x++;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

In this rewritten test, a `ReentrantLock` is used to synchronize access to the shared variable `x`. The `lock()` method is called before incrementing `x`, and the `unlock()` method is called in a `finally` block to ensure the lock is always released, even if an exception occurs. This ensures that only one thread can increment `x` at a time, preventing race conditions.

